WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.940
DEREK DOWNEY: Hi.

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:03.840
I'm Derek Downey, a developer
advocate for Google Cloud.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.970
I am currently working
on developing a banking

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.540
application that leverages Cloud
Spanner, the highly-scalable,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.910
managed relational
database from Google Cloud.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.256
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:20.030
In the previous video, I set
up a regional Spanner instance

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.000
with a small amount
of compute capacity

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.460
for developing my
banking application.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.700
I also created a customers
database on the instance.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:32.390
And all the tables related to my
app will live in this database.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:36.010
Now, my company might have other
internal-only data completely

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.630
unrelated to my banking app.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.180
That data could be placed
in a separate database,

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.080
because Spanner instances
can have multiple databases.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.490
Databases are a way to organize
related tables together

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:53.180
and to provide additional access
control to the data stored

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:54.570
on the instance.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.350
For example, my banking
app should definitely not

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.380
have access to the
internal database.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.660
Besides access
control, each database

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.790
can be configured with
different options that

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:07.500
affect the
performance, recovery,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.660
and security of your database.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.180
These options control
the optimizer version,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.430
version retention period,
and database encryption.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.820
The first database option
is the optimizer version.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.270
The optimizer's job is to
create an execution plan that

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.750
is an efficient way to retrieve
data from Spanner based

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.820
on the given SQL query.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.730
It has a direct impact on
performance of requests

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:34.140
issued to your database.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.270
Over time, the
query optimizer will

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.810
change to improve the accuracy
of the estimates required

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.180
to create this execution plan.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.910
When Spanner releases a
new optimizer version,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.250
the default behavior
is for databases

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:51.470
to use the new version
30 days after release.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.780
Optimizer versions can
change the performance

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.460
of your query workload.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.860
So it's important to
compare query performance

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.390
of the new version
with the older version.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.160
You can pin the
optimizer version

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.500
a database uses to
a specific version

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.419
by altering the database.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:10.970
Altering the database
optimizer version to null

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.520
resets to the default
behavior of updating

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.055
30 days after the
release of a new version.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.650
You can also change
the optimizer version

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.920
for individual queries by
providing the optimizer version

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.120
query hunt.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.190
This is useful to
validate queries

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:30.310
where stable performance is
critical without changing

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.710
the optimizer version
for the entire database.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.570
Another important
database option

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.030
is the version
retention period, which

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.630
affects the ability to perform
a point in time recovery.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.550
By default, Spanner
will maintain

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.520
all versions of the data
and schema for one hour.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.290
In my banking
application, I want

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.110
to protect against
accidental data loss.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.910
With Spanner's
default retention,

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.790
I have an hour to copy
the data as it existed

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.100
before an accidental
change and then

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.760
load it back into the database.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.660
During normal
business operations,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.390
this hour might be enough.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.620
But what about during
off hours or the weekend?

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.580
I might not be able to respond
in time to avoid data loss.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.120
So I want to be sure to
size my version retention

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.420
period to give my team enough
time to recover outside

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.130
of normal hours.

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:23.890
One way to do this would be
to set the version retention

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.840
period according to
my backup frequency.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.550
For instance, if I take a backup
of my database once a day, then

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:35.690
I will want to set my version
retention period to one day.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.060
The last option I will
cover is how encryption is

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.370
handled for Spanner databases.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.100
All data in Spanner
is encrypted at rest.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.470
By default, this encryption
is handled for you

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.270
by Google Cloud.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.430
However, if you have
specific compliance

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:52.880
or regulatory requirements on
the keys encrypting the data,

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.880
Spanner offers the ability
to deploy your own encryption

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.210
keys.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.820
These are called Customer
Managed Encryption Keys,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.210
and are managed through
Google Cloud KMS.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.880
It is possible to have
some databases configured

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.300
with customer encryption
keys, while other databases

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.700
on the same instance use Google
Cloud to manage encryption.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.230
Setting up and
managing these custom

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.930
encryption keys is beyond
the scope of this video.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.570
But there are some important
pieces of information

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.000
when using custom
encryption keys.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.370
First, a database
must be created

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.200
with the custom encryption key.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.350
Custom encryption keys cannot
be enabled on an existing

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:32.150
database.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.730
Second, for backups,
the primary version

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.850
of the encryption key at
the time of backup creation

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:41.270
is used to encrypt backups.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:44.990
Finally, encryption keys
must be available to decrypt

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:46.850
the database and backups.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.490
If a key is deleted
and can't be recovered,

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:52.820
any Spanner database or
backup encrypted with that key

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.530
is unrecoverable.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.500
For now, I will be using the
default encryption managed

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:00.820
by Google Cloud for my
banking application.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.880
Cloud Spanner offers the
ability to organize related data

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:06.730
into multiple databases
on your Spanner instance.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.740
This provides some
additional control

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.620
over who has access to
the data stored there.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.530
In this video, I went
over some other options

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.500
that give you more control
over the performance, recovery,

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.780
and security of your
Spanner database.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:22.450
In our next video, I
will go into more detail

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.670
about the Spanner
schema, including

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:28.510
data types, data organization,
and schema management.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:31.120
In the meantime, please
let us know in the comments

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.100
if you have any questions
around database specific options

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:34.600
in Spanner.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

